# Target C-II — joint dominant/complement endpoint retention spends outer detour once

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Repository base:** `410f184e576bb8a431928047637503039b9899c5`  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the direct dominant/complement energy tradeoff  
**Scope:** full-core/no-container Type-II branch  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The hostile-audited direct energy tradeoff treats the dominant atomic outer occurrences
and the diameter-braced complement outer-to-outer train endpoints as disjoint energy
supports, but it truncates the two populations to loaded-hull side-length capacity
**separately**. Each truncation is allowed to discard as much as the full outer detour
`d_out`.

That is unnecessary. The two populations are disjoint subsets of the same
occurrence-counted outer contact walk. Truncate their union once, route by route. The
single total discard is at most `d_out`, and the retained dominant mass `z` plus the
retained complement-pair mass `p` satisfies

\[
\boxed{
z+p\ge \pi-N_*,
}
\tag{JR.1}

where `N_*` is the same complement-train-count upper bound as in the direct theorem.
The actual dominant terminal count cancels completely.

The resulting energy obstruction raises the safe proof-only floor from `1.83236` to

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q-k\over D}\ge1.83279.
}
\tag{JR.2}

The numerical scalar crossing is about

\[
1.832793564819059\ldots .
\]

Only the round value `1.83279` is claimed.

---

## 1. Current scalar inputs

Use

\[
t={q-k\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},
\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D},
\]

and

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0050.
\]

The current low-`t` network gives

\[
C_0\le C={T-q/2\over D}
\le
U:={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-\kappa/2.
\tag{JR.3}
\]

Thus

\[
2\delta+\kappa/2
\le R(t):={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0.
\tag{JR.4}
\]

Freeze the same dominant exact `pi/3` atomic orbit as in the audited direct tradeoff.
Let `K` be its outer terminal occurrence density and let `N_c` be the complement train
density. The same common-`C` argument gives

\[
N_c\le
N_*:=
U+{t+\kappa\over2}-{4\Delta_2\over U}.
\tag{JR.5}
\]

No lower or upper bound on `K` will be needed below.

---

## 2. Raw complement two-outer pair count

The full outer-face occurrence density is

\[
\pi+\delta.
\]

After assigning `K` occurrences to dominant-window trains, complement trains carry

\[
\pi+\delta-K
\]

outer occurrences. If `P_c` complement trains have two outer terminal occurrences,
then every other complement train contributes at most one outer occurrence, so

\[
\pi+\delta-K\le N_c+P_c.
\]

Using (JR.5),

\[
\boxed{
P_c\ge \pi+\delta-K-N_*.
}
\tag{JR.6}

This is exactly the raw pair count used in the direct tradeoff.

---

## 3. Joint routewise retention lemma

For each distinguished Section-63 route `W_i`, write

\[
\ell_i=s_i+d_i,
\]

where `s_i` is the physical loaded-hull side length and `d_i>=0` is the route detour.
The routes partition the occurrence-counted outer walk and

\[
\sum_i d_i=d_{out}.
\tag{JR.7}

On route `i`, select two disjoint sets of outer edge occurrences:

1. all dominant-window outer terminal occurrences;
2. all terminal occurrences belonging to complement outer-to-outer trains counted by
   `P_c`.

They are disjoint because every outer edge occurrence belongs to exactly one complete
stopped train, and the dominant/complement train classes are disjoint. Hence the total
selected occurrence count on `W_i` is at most `ell_i`.

Assign fractional weights in `[0,1]` to the union so that the total retained occurrence
weight on route `i` is at most `s_i`. This can be done while discarding no more than

\[
(\#\text{selected on }W_i-s_i)_+
\le \ell_i-s_i=d_i.
\tag{JR.8}

Let

- `x` be the total discarded dominant occurrence weight divided by `D`;
- `y` be the total discarded complement endpoint weight divided by `D`.

Summing (JR.8) gives the single budget

\[
\boxed{x+y\le\delta.}
\tag{JR.9}

The retained dominant occurrence mass therefore satisfies

\[
z\ge K-x.
\tag{JR.10}

For one complement two-outer train let its two retained endpoint weights be `a,b` and
retain that pair with weight `min(a,b)`. Since

\[
1-\min(a,b)\le(1-a)+(1-b),
\]

the total complement **pair** mass loses at most the discarded complement endpoint
weight. Thus

\[
p\ge P_c-y.
\tag{JR.11}

Combining (JR.6), (JR.9)--(JR.11),

\[
\begin{aligned}
z+p
&\ge K+P_c-(x+y)\\
&\ge K+(\pi+\delta-K-N_*)-\delta.
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
z+p\ge \pi-N_*.}
\tag{JR.12}

This is the new finite bookkeeping gain. The direct theorem retained only the weaker
total `pi-N_*-delta` because it allowed each population to spend the full detour
capacity independently.

Bridges cause no problem: outer-face occurrences, rather than abstract physical edges,
are counted in `ell_i`, so the two appearances of a bridge are distinct capacity
occurrences exactly as in the hostile-audited parent theorem.

---

## 4. The two energy lower bounds still add

The joint retention has total weight at most `s_i` on every route. Consequently each
retained subpopulation separately has weight at most `s_i` there.

For dominant-window occurrences, the same round-hull projective bathtub theorem gives

\[
\boxed{E_{dom}\ge F(z),
\qquad F(z)=z-6\sin(z/6).}
\tag{JR.13}

For complement pairs, both retained endpoint measures are separately dominated by the
physical loaded-hull perimeter measure. The same three-sector noncrossing/bracing
theorem therefore gives, for `0<=p<=1`,

\[
\boxed{E_{comp}\ge J(p),
\qquad J(p)=2p-6\sin(p/3).}
\tag{JR.14}

The dominant and complement occurrence supports remain disjoint, so the actual outer
route energy satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\delta\ge F(z)+J(p).
}
\tag{JR.15}

If a retained complement pair mass exceeds one, retaining only unit mass gives
`J(1)=0.0368318...`, already larger than the entire detour range used below. Hence a
survivor in the minimizing regime has `p<1`.

---

## 5. Eliminate the unknown dominant count by infimal convolution

Put

\[
S:=\pi-N_*.
\tag{JR.16}

Both `F` and `J` are increasing on the relevant positive ranges, so (JR.12)--(JR.15)
give

\[
\boxed{
\delta\ge H(S),
\qquad
H(S):=\min_{\substack{z,p\ge0\\z+p=S}}
\{F(z)+J(p)\}.
}
\tag{JR.17}

Thus `K` disappears from the scalar problem.

On the present range the minimum is unique and interior. Indeed

\[
F''(z)={1\over6}\sin(z/6)>0,
\qquad
J''(p)={2\over3}\sin(p/3)>0,
\]

so the split objective is strictly convex. Its minimizer satisfies

\[
\boxed{F'(z)=J'(p),
\qquad z+p=S.}
\tag{JR.18}

The current interval has `N_*>0.9225`, hence `S<2.22`. At `p=0.72` one has
`z<1.50`, and

\[
F'(1.50)<J'(0.72).
\]

Therefore the unique minimizing pair has

\[
0<p<0.72<1.
\tag{JR.19}

This also keeps (JR.14) strictly inside its smooth regime.

Write

\[
\lambda(S):=H'(S)=F'(z)=J'(p)>0
\tag{JR.20}

at the unique minimizer.

---

## 6. The feasible triangle again reduces to `kappa=0`, maximal detour

Since

\[
N_*=U+{t+\kappa\over2}-{4\Delta_2\over U},
\]

direct differentiation gives

\[
{\partial N_*\over\partial\kappa}
=-{2\Delta_2\over U^2}<0.
\tag{JR.21}

Hence

\[
{\partial S\over\partial\kappa}
={2\Delta_2\over U^2}>0,
\]

and therefore

\[
{\partial\over\partial\kappa}[H(S)-\delta]
=\lambda(S){2\Delta_2\over U^2}>0.
\tag{JR.22}

The weakest point has

\[
\boxed{\kappa=0.}
\tag{JR.23}

Similarly,

\[
{\partial S\over\partial\delta}
=2\left(1+{4\Delta_2\over U^2}\right)<4,
\tag{JR.24}

because `U>=C0>2sqrt(Delta2)`. By (JR.19),

\[
\lambda(S)=J'(p)<J'(0.72)<0.058.
\]

Thus

\[
{\partial\over\partial\delta}[H(S)-\delta]
<4(0.058)-1<0.
\tag{JR.25}

The weakest point is maximal detour. With (JR.23), the network boundary gives

\[
\boxed{
\delta={R(t)\over2},
\qquad U=C_0.
}
\tag{JR.26}

---

## 7. One-variable boundary problem

At (JR.26),

\[
\delta_0(t)
={1\over2}\left({9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0\right),
\tag{JR.27}

\[
N_0(t)=C_0+{t\over2}-{4\Delta_2\over C_0},
\tag{JR.28}

and

\[
S_0(t)=\pi-N_0(t).
\tag{JR.29}

Let `(z_0,p_0)` be the unique solution of

\[
z_0+p_0=S_0(t),
\qquad
F'(z_0)=J'(p_0).
\tag{JR.30}

Define

\[
G_0(t)=F(z_0)+J(p_0)-\delta_0(t).
\tag{JR.31}

The envelope theorem gives a particularly simple monotonicity formula:

\[
G_0'(t)
=H'(S_0)S_0'(t)-\delta_0'(t)
=-{1\over2}\lambda(S_0)-{9\over4}<0.
\tag{JR.32}

Thus there is at most one scalar crossing.

At the advertised safe endpoint `t=1.83279`, the independent numerical calculation
gives approximately

\[
\begin{aligned}
\delta_0&=0.02737203954625,\\
S_0&=2.21521074516116,\\
z_0&=1.63725469315688,\\
p_0&=0.57795605200428,\\
F(z_0)&=0.02024314255598,\\
J(p_0)&=0.00713698378252,
\end{aligned}
\]

so

\[
\boxed{G_0(1.83279)>8.0\times10^{-6}.}
\tag{JR.33}

The scalar zero is

\[
1.832793564819059\ldots .
\tag{JR.34}

The companion Decimal certificate supplies the load-bearing root bracket and interval
checks.

---

## 8. Frontier effect

The gain over the direct tradeoff is small numerically but structurally clean:

```text
old retention:
    dominant may discard delta
    complement may discard delta

joint retention:
    dominant + complement endpoint union discards at most delta total
```

The actual dominant terminal count no longer needs to be optimized. The only split left
is how the retained total `pi-N_*` is distributed between the two disjoint energy
mechanisms, and strict convexity solves that split exactly.

The resulting proof-only floor is

\[
\boxed{\liminf(q-k)/D\ge1.83279.}
\]

C-II remains open. The next loss is no longer outer endpoint capacity. It is the
three-sector complement bracing bound itself: `J(p)` uses only that phase intervals of
width `pi/3` are noncrossing and ignores the actual complement phase measure and its
crossings with the dominant atomic orbit.

---

## 9. Reproducibility

Run

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_joint_dominant_complement_endpoint_retention.py
```

for the high-precision scalar checks.
